Alright, let's implement the solution. The standard Exercism phone number solution:

```python
class PhoneNumber:
    def __init__(self, number):
        self.number = self._clean(number)

    @staticmethod
    def _clean(number):
        # Strip allowed characters: digits, spaces, parentheses, dashes, dots, plus
        allowed = set("0123456789 .()-+")
        for c in number:
            if c not in allowed:
                if c.isalpha():
                    raise ValueError("letters not permitted")
                raise ValueError("punctuations not permitted")
        digits = "".join(c for c in number if c.isdigit())
        if len(digits) == 11:
            if digits[0] != "1":
                raise ValueError("11 digits must start with 1")
            digits = digits[1:]
        if len(digits) < 10:
            raise ValueError("must not be fewer than 10 digits")
        if len(digits) > 11:
            raise ValueError("must not be greater than 11 digits")
        if digits[0] == "0":
            raise ValueError("area code cannot start with zero")
        if digits[0] == "1":
            raise ValueError("area code cannot start with one")
        if digits[3] == "0":
            raise ValueError("exchange code cannot start with zero")
        if digits[3] == "1":
            raise ValueError("exchange code cannot start with one")
        return digits
```

Wait a moment, about the order of error checks: in the case of "321234567890" (12 digits) — the test expects "must not be greater than 11 digits". And "123456789" (9 digits) expects "must not be fewer than 10 digits". In the 11-digit case: if it doesn't start with 1 → "11 digits must start with 1". After stripping the leading 1, it becomes 10 digits.

Note: the test "invalid_with_punctuations" uses "523-@:!-7890" — @ : ! are punctuation. Since the allowed set includes - and ( and ), @:! will raise "punctuations not permitted". Good.

Order of checks: first check for letters/punctuation (character by character). Then length.

Edge case: the 11-digit length check — after stripping the country code, if the length is 10, no problem. If it's greater than 11, raise "must not be greater than 11 digits". If less than 10 (and not 11), raise "must not be fewer than 10 digits".

Also, the area code / exchange code checks are done after the length checks.

The `number` property: the test accesses `.number` as an attribute. Store the cleaned digits in self.number. area_code property, pretty method.

Alright, let's write it.

